ientists in the uk believe the b-117  variant originally came from a single  immunocompromised patient a person who  would have had a harder time  fighting kova 19 making them a vessel  for the virus to incubate  and mutate tonight pharah nasser  explores how effective the vaccines are  against any new mutations and this is  the virus spike protein here  even world-renowned expert ravi gupta  was surprised by how the novel  coronavirus  started to change you know we'd been  we'd been learning for  a year that this virus didn't change it  was always the same there were a few  mutations here and there  and yet in this person we saw just the  most dramatic shifts you could imagine  as the variant from the uk started to  take hold gupta saw a patient  sick with covid for more than a hundred  days  his body weakened by previous  chemotherapy the virus not only wouldn't  leave  it kept replicating again and again  when we discovered this there was  nothing known about variants we didn't  know that you could find  viruses with so many mutations in one  virus gupta and a team of researchers at  the university of cambridge took  continuous samples as the virus mutated  in the man's body  but one of the mutations we found led us  to discover the b117 variant because  this mutation in our patient that had  emerged in response to treatment  actually was one of the key mutations  found in the b117  the patient sadly died but from this  case  scientists gained important clues about  what we know now as the more infectious  b117 variant and it's 23 mutations  there are a growing number of cases  showing evidence of immunocompromised  covid19 patients  accelerating viral evolution now  research is being done  to see if those with low immunity will  be protected by the covid vaccines  this also raises questions about  vaccines overall  what is the research showing about how  effective these vaccines are  against the variants so if you look in  in certain kind of  test tube experiments you'll see that  the variant from south africa for  example  does have a reduced response to the  vaccines  especially astrazeneca but if you look  more closely you still have  way more antibody than you need to  neutralize it vaccine manufacturers are  exploring booster shots and other ways  to bolster covid19 vaccines against  variants of concern  but for now it's a race against the  viruses sequencing superpowers  and scientists rushing to keep up with  it  farah nasser global news toronto  there are a growing number of variant  cases tomorrow in the new reality we  explore how that's possible  that's at 7 pm with donna friesen right  here on global still ahead netflix won't  chill  the streaming giant is on the watch for  password freeloaders  you're watching global national  [Music]  an ice cleaner for the vancouver canucks  is sweeping the nation with his moves  his skills went viral after the nhl  posted the performance online  the canucks say the sweeper named devin  has been with the team for four years  fans joked the team could use his energy  during the game  not between breaks ouch harry and  megan's popularity has  taken a hit for the prince it's an  all-time low in the aftermath of that  interview with oprah winfrey  where the couple shared what their life  is like inside the royal family  and claims of racism according to a poll  done in the uk  48 percent now hold a negative attitude  towards harry  while 45 have a positive view of the  prince  and 58 say they have a negative opinion  of megan  if you've been using someone else's  netflix account you may need to get your  own  the streaming service is testing ways to  crack down on password sharing  some users in several countries  including canada are getting pop-up  messages like this  if you don't live with the owner of this  account you need your own account to  keep watching  netflix says the test is being done for  security reasons  the stay at home reporter up next mike  drolet gets  personal about the pandemic  living life during a pandemic has been  hard on all of us  tonight mike delay reflects on how his  own family got through these  difficult times i haven't been down this  driveway  in a year 12 months of working virtually  hasn't been easy  but i'm one of the lucky ones who's kept  his job and stayed healthy  when the pandemic hit the furthest thing  from my mind was documenting my life  where i see me is in the stories i tell  about others  so this is what i made oh nicely done  i'm impressed mike from the outset we  looked for distractions  he's running the bases the pandemic  puppy helped fill a void  and brought some much needed laughter  into our lives but the isolation was  unavoidable  virtual school zoom hangout with friends  all of it made us want to be  more attentive parents it's refreshing  in many ways the pandemic year was the  reset on our priorities that we needed  everyone gets distracted be it from work  or just life in general  and with an only child the fear was how  the toxicity of 2020  would shape her going forward you see we  have a precocious eight-year-old  the last thing we want is for her to  fear the world  but on the flip side we didn't want to  shelter her from what was going on  in those times when i needed clarity i'd  jump on my bike and hit the trails  and no i never really found it and maybe  that's the lesson i've learned this year  don't overthink anything enjoy what we  have  knowing that others have endured more  hardship and that this virus  has taken so many lives  because the ballet studios will open  again soon  at least my little ballerina will be  ready  micro by global news toronto  very sweet story that is global national  for this friday night i'm robin gill  tonight your canada is peggy's cove  in nova scotia we would love to see your  corner of the country so please keep  sending your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a 